{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-canaries","statement":"The React Canaries post was written by Dan Abramov, Sophie Alpert, Rick Hanlon, Sebastian Markbage, and Andrew Clark, and published May 3, 2023.","source_span":"May 3, 2023 by Dan Abramov, Sophie Alpert, Rick Hanlon, Sebastian Markbåge, and Andrew Clark"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-canaries","statement":"React is introducing a new officially supported Canary release channel.","source_span":"We are introducing a new officially supported Canary release channel."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-canaries","statement":"Canaries let developers start using individual new React features before they land in the semver-stable releases.","source_span":"Canaries let you start using individual new React features before they land in the semver-stable releases."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-canaries","statement":"Unlike the Experimental channel, React Canaries only include features the team reasonably believes to be ready for adoption.","source_span":"Unlike the Experimental channel, React Canaries only include features that we reasonably believe to be ready for adoption."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-canaries","statement":"React continues to follow semver for every Stable release.","source_span":"As always, React continues to follow semver for every Stable release."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-canaries","statement":"Typical React feature development goes through five stages: experimental_/unstable_ prefix in the experimental channel, testing with a Meta team, prefix removal and default availability on the main branch, posting an RFC, then writing documentation and releasing in a stable release.","source_span":"Typically, every React feature has gone through the same stages: 1. We develop an initial version and prefix it with experimental_ or unstable_... 5. When we are close to cutting an open source release, we write documentation for the feature and finally release the feature in a stable React release."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-canaries","statement":"Every stable release of React Native is pinned to a specific commit from the main branch of the React repository.","source_span":"Every stable release of React Native is pinned to a specific commit from the main branch of the React repository."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-canaries","statement":"TC39, the JavaScript standards committee, handles changes in numbered stages, a process the Canaries workflow resembles.","source_span":"This workflow is closer to how TC39, the JavaScript standards committee, handles changes in numbered stages."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-canaries","statement":"The React team recommends against using Experimental releases in production because experimental APIs can undergo significant breaking changes or be removed entirely.","source_span":"we recommend against using them in production because experimental APIs can undergo significant breaking changes on their way to stabilization (or can even be removed entirely)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-canaries","statement":"Going forward, the React team plans to announce significant breaking changes in Canary releases on their blog.","source_span":"going forward we plan to announce any significant breaking changes in Canaries on our blog"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-canaries","fact_refs":[0],"question":"Who authored the React Canaries blog post and when was it published?","answer":"It was written by Dan Abramov, Sophie Alpert, Rick Hanlon, Sebastian Markbage, and Andrew Clark, and published on May 3, 2023."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-canaries","fact_refs":[1,2],"question":"What is the purpose of the new Canary release channel React introduced?","answer":"It lets curated setups like frameworks adopt individual new React features before those features land in a stable semver release."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-canaries","fact_refs":[3,9],"question":"How does the Canary channel differ from the Experimental channel, and what will React do if a Canary breaking change lands?","answer":"Canary only includes features the team reasonably believes are ready for adoption, unlike Experimental. Going forward, the team plans to announce any significant breaking changes in Canary releases on their blog."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-canaries","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What are the typical stages a React feature goes through before reaching a stable release?","answer":"It starts prefixed with experimental_ or unstable_ in the experimental channel, gets tested with a Meta team, has its prefix removed once available by default on the main branch, gets an RFC, and finally gets documentation and ships in a stable release."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-canaries","fact_refs":[6],"question":"How does React Native's release process relate to the React main branch?","answer":"Every stable release of React Native is pinned to a specific commit from the main branch of the React repository."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-canaries","fact_refs":[4],"question":"Does React still follow semantic versioning for stable releases after introducing Canaries?","answer":"Yes, React continues to follow semver for every Stable release."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-canaries","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What existing JavaScript standards process is the Canaries workflow compared to?","answer":"It's compared to TC39, the JavaScript standards committee, which handles changes in numbered stages."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-labs-what-we-have-been-working-on-february-20","statement":"The \"React Labs: What We've Been Working On – February 2024\" post was authored by Joseph Savona, Ricky Hanlon, Andrew Clark, Matt Carroll, and Dan Abramov, published February 15, 2024.","source_span":"February 15, 2024 by Joseph Savona, Ricky Hanlon, Andrew Clark, Matt Carroll, and Dan Abramov."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-labs-what-we-have-been-working-on-february-20","statement":"React Compiler now powers instagram.com in production.","source_span":"React Compiler is no longer a research project: the compiler now powers instagram.com in production"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-labs-what-we-have-been-working-on-february-20","statement":"React Compiler compiles code safely by modeling both the rules of JavaScript and the \"rules of React,\" such as components needing to be idempotent and not mutate props or state.","source_span":"React Compiler is able to compile code safely by modeling both the rules of JavaScript and the \"rules of React\". For example, React components must be idempotent — returning the same value given the same inputs — and can’t mutate props or state values."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-labs-what-we-have-been-working-on-february-20","statement":"The React team recommends enabling Strict Mode and configuring React's ESLint plugin to catch subtle bugs and prepare code for React Compiler.","source_span":"we recommend enabling Strict Mode and configuring React’s ESLint plugin. These tools can help to catch subtle bugs in your React code"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-labs-what-we-have-been-working-on-february-20","statement":"Actions allow passing a function to DOM elements such as <form/>, e.g. <form action={search}>.","source_span":"Actions allow you to pass a function to DOM elements such as <form/>"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-labs-what-we-have-been-working-on-february-20","statement":"By default, Actions are submitted within a transition, keeping the current page interactive while the action is processing.","source_span":"By default, Actions are submitted within a transition, keeping the current page interactive while the action is processing."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-labs-what-we-have-been-working-on-february-20","statement":"useOptimistic is a feature for managing optimistic state updates, letting you apply temporary updates that are automatically reverted once the final state commits.","source_span":"we're introducing a feature named useOptimistic for managing optimistic state updates. With this hook, you can apply temporary updates that are automatically reverted once the final state commits."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-labs-what-we-have-been-working-on-february-20","statement":"React provides useFormStatus and useActionState hooks to access the current state and response of a form action.","source_span":"providing hooks like useFormStatus, and useActionState to access the current state and response of the form action"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-labs-what-we-have-been-working-on-february-20","statement":"Actions can be defined on the client with standard JavaScript or on the server with the 'use server' directive.","source_span":"You can define them on the client side using standard JavaScript or on the server with the 'use server' directive."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-labs-what-we-have-been-working-on-february-20","fact_refs":[1],"question":"Where is React Compiler currently running in production according to this February 2024 update?","answer":"React Compiler now powers instagram.com in production."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-labs-what-we-have-been-working-on-february-20","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What two sets of rules does React Compiler model in order to safely compile code?","answer":"It models both the rules of JavaScript and the \"rules of React,\" such as the requirement that components be idempotent and not mutate props or state."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-labs-what-we-have-been-working-on-february-20","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What do the React team recommend developers enable to help prepare for React Compiler?","answer":"They recommend enabling Strict Mode and configuring React's ESLint plugin, which help catch subtle bugs and future-proof apps for React Compiler."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-labs-what-we-have-been-working-on-february-20","fact_refs":[4,5],"question":"How can you pass a function to a form element with React Actions, and what happens to the page while the action runs?","answer":"You pass a function via the action prop, e.g. <form action={search}>; by default the Action is submitted within a transition, keeping the current page interactive while it processes."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-labs-what-we-have-been-working-on-february-20","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What does the useOptimistic hook do in React?","answer":"It lets you apply temporary optimistic state updates that are automatically reverted once the final state commits."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-labs-what-we-have-been-working-on-february-20","fact_refs":[7],"question":"Which hooks give access to the current state and response of a form action in React?","answer":"useFormStatus and useActionState."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-labs-what-we-have-been-working-on-february-20","fact_refs":[8],"question":"How can Actions be defined on the server side in React?","answer":"They can be defined on the server using the 'use server' directive."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-19-upgrade-guide","statement":"The React 19 Upgrade Guide was authored by Ricky Hanlon, published April 25, 2024.","source_span":"April 25, 2024 by Ricky Hanlon"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-19-upgrade-guide","statement":"React also published react@18.3, which is identical to 18.2 but adds warnings for deprecated APIs and other changes needed for React 19.","source_span":"we've published a react@18.3 release that is identical to 18.2 but adds warnings for deprecated APIs and other changes that are needed for React 19"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-19-upgrade-guide","statement":"The React team recommends upgrading to React 18.3 first to help identify issues before upgrading to React 19.","source_span":"We recommend upgrading to React 18.3 first to help identify any issues before upgrading to React 19."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-19-upgrade-guide","statement":"To install the latest React 19 and React DOM via npm, run: npm install --save-exact react@^19.0.0 react-dom@^19.0.0.","source_span":"npm install --save-exact react@^19.0.0 react-dom@^19.0.0"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-19-upgrade-guide","statement":"React 19 requires the new JSX transform that was introduced in 2020.","source_span":"In React 19, we're adding additional improvements like using ref as a prop and JSX speed improvements that require the new transform."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-19-upgrade-guide","statement":"React 19 codemods can be run all at once with: npx codemod@latest react/19/migration-recipe.","source_span":"npx codemod@latest react/19/migration-recipe"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-19-upgrade-guide","statement":"In React 19, errors during render are not re-thrown: uncaught errors are reported to window.reportError, and errors caught by an Error Boundary are reported to console.error.","source_span":"Uncaught Errors: Errors that are not caught by an Error Boundary are reported to window.reportError. Caught Errors: Errors that are caught by an Error Boundary are reported to console.error."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-19-upgrade-guide","statement":"React 19 added onUncaughtError and onCaughtError options to createRoot and hydrateRoot for custom error handling.","source_span":"we've added new methods to createRoot and hydrateRoot for custom error handling"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-19-upgrade-guide","statement":"PropTypes were deprecated in April 2017 (v15.5.0).","source_span":"PropTypes were deprecated in April 2017 (v15.5.0)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-19-upgrade-guide","statement":"In React 19, propType checks are removed from the React package and using propTypes will be silently ignored.","source_span":"In React 19, we're removing the propType checks from the React package, and using them will be silently ignored."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-19-upgrade-guide","fact_refs":[0],"question":"Who wrote the React 19 Upgrade Guide and when was it published?","answer":"Ricky Hanlon wrote it, and it was published on April 25, 2024."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-19-upgrade-guide","fact_refs":[1,2],"question":"What is react@18.3 and why did the React team recommend upgrading to it first?","answer":"react@18.3 is a release identical to React 18.2 but with added warnings for deprecated APIs and other React 19-related changes; the team recommends upgrading to it first to help identify issues before moving to React 19."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-19-upgrade-guide","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What npm command installs React 19 and React DOM 19?","answer":"npm install --save-exact react@^19.0.0 react-dom@^19.0.0."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-19-upgrade-guide","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What command runs all the React 19 migration codemods at once?","answer":"npx codemod@latest react/19/migration-recipe."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-19-upgrade-guide","fact_refs":[6],"question":"How are errors during render handled differently in React 19 compared to previous versions?","answer":"In React 19, render errors are no longer re-thrown: errors not caught by an Error Boundary are reported to window.reportError, and errors caught by an Error Boundary are reported to console.error."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-19-upgrade-guide","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What new options were added to createRoot and hydrateRoot in React 19 for custom error handling?","answer":"onUncaughtError and onCaughtError."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-19-upgrade-guide","fact_refs":[8,9],"question":"When were PropTypes deprecated, and what happens when you use them in React 19?","answer":"PropTypes were deprecated in April 2017 (v15.5.0); in React 19 the propType checks are removed from the React package, so using propTypes is silently ignored."}
